<section class="page-hero"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/family-home.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/family-home-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/family-home.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="684" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.12"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>›</span><a href="/appraisal-solutions/">Services</a><span>›</span>Home Insurance Appraisal</nav><span class="kicker light up">Appraisal service</span><h1 class="up" style="animation-delay:.06s">Home Insurance Appraisal for Wisconsin Homeowners</h1><p class="lede up" style="animation-delay:.14s">Homes here are built for hard winters and repaired by trades who know them. When an insurer’s estimate doesn’t reflect how your house was actually built, appraisal gives you a structured way to close the gap.</p><div class="btn-row up" style="animation-delay:.22s"><a class="btn gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn ghost-l" href="/contact-us/">Free claim review</a></div><div class="facts up" style="animation-delay:.3s"><div><small>Property types</small><b>Houses, flats, condos, cabins</b></div><div><small>Typical dispute</small><b>Scope and pricing</b></div><div><small>Starts with</small><b>Your policy’s appraisal clause</b></div></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<p>Home insurance appraisal in Wisconsin is for homeowners who agree with their insurer that a loss is covered but cannot agree on what it will cost to fix. We serve as your appraiser under the policy’s appraisal clause, re-scope the damage independently and work toward a binding figure for the amount of loss.</p>
<h2>The houses we see most</h2>
<h3>Single-family homes</h3>
<p>From ranch homes in Waukesha County to older plaster-and-lath two-stories in Madison’s near east side, single-family claims make up most of our work. Older homes raise questions newer estimating templates handle badly: true plaster repair versus drywall over lath, old-growth trim that has to be milled, and wiring that cannot be disturbed without triggering code work.</p>
<h3>Duplexes and Polish flats</h3>
<p>Milwaukee’s upper-and-lower duplexes and raised “Polish flats” bring their own puzzles. Two kitchens, two baths, shared stairwells and sometimes Cream City brick that can’t be matched off the shelf. If one unit is a rental, loss of rents and tenant contents may also be in play, depending on how the policy is written.</p>
<h3>Condo units</h3>
<p>Unit owners usually carry a policy that picks up where the association’s master policy stops. Disputes often hinge on where that line falls, so we read both the declarations and the bylaws before pricing anything. For claims on the building itself, see our <a href="/appraisal-solutions/commercial-hoa-appraisal/">association appraisal</a> page.</p>
<h3>Cottages and cabins Up North</h3>
<p>Seasonal places in Vilas, Oneida and Door counties are often damaged while nobody is there. Remote locations push labor and travel costs up, and a contractor willing to drive two hours to a lake road charges for it. Estimates built on metro pricing tend to miss that.</p>
<h2>Where homeowner estimates usually fall apart</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Matching.</strong> Replacing one slope of shingles or one wall of siding when the material is discontinued.</li>
<li><strong>Interior finishes.</strong> Painting a patch rather than the full wall or room, or replacing only part of a continuous floor.</li>
<li><strong>Code items.</strong> Ice-and-water shield, egress, electrical and other upgrades your municipality now requires.</li>
<li><strong>Overhead and profit.</strong> Left off even when several trades are clearly needed.</li>
<li><strong>Depreciation.</strong> Applied too aggressively to items that were in sound condition.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Our process for a homeowner claim</h2>
<p>We start with a phone call and a look at your paperwork. If appraisal looks like a fit, we inspect the house in person, build our own line-item estimate and compare it to the carrier’s. Once you or the insurer demands appraisal, we take the appointment and handle the back-and-forth with the other appraiser.</p>
<p>Want a rough sense of whether the gap is worth pursuing? Try the <a href="/insurance-claim-calculators/">claim calculators</a>, or find an appraiser near you through our <a href="/local-appraisers/">local appraisers</a> directory, including <a href="/local-appraisers/milwaukee/">Milwaukee</a> and <a href="/local-appraisers/madison/">Madison</a>.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Plain talk</b>We can’t promise an outcome. Appraisal can raise, confirm or occasionally lower the number. What we can promise is an estimate built from the house itself, not from a template, and an honest read on whether the gap justifies the cost of appraisal.</div>

<h2>Common questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Is appraisal worth it for a smaller homeowner claim?</summary><div class="a"><p>It depends on the size of the gap compared with the cost of appraisal. If the difference is modest, an estimate review and a supplement request may be the better first step.</p></div></details><details><summary>My condo association and I disagree about who covers the damage. Can appraisal help?</summary><div class="a"><p>Appraisal sets the amount of a loss, not who is responsible for it. Responsibility usually turns on the bylaws and the two policies, which may call for an attorney’s input.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do you handle rental units in a duplex I live in?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes. Owner-occupied duplexes and flats are common here, and we price both units and note any rental-related items your policy covers.</p></div></details></div>
